(IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA) ' 
(CIVIL APPELLATE JURISDICTION) ' 
(CIVIL APPEAL NO. 4582 OF 2021) ' 
(\(Arising out of SLP \(C\) No. 12903 of 2019\)) ' 
(M/S. BHARAT INFRASTRUCTURE LIMITED                     ... APPELLANT\(S\)) ' 
(VERSUS) ' 
(STATE OF MAHARASHTRA & ANR.                             ... RESPONDENT\(S\)) ' 
(JUDGMENT) ' 
(DR. D.Y. CHANDRACHUD, J.) ' 
(1. Leave granted. This appeal arises from a final judgment and order dated 14 March 2019 passed by the Division Bench of the High Court of Judicature at Bombay in Writ Petition No. 2891 of 2017, whereby the High Court dismissed the challenge instituted by the appellant against an order of termination of a public infrastructure procurement contract issued by the Maharashtra State Road Development Corporation.) ' 
(2. The factual background matrix reveals that on 12 January 2016, a tender notice was published for the construction of a four-lane elevated highway bypass spanning 18.5 kilometers in Pune district. The appellant was declared the successful bidder, and a Concession Agreement was executed on 28 April 2016 with a stipulated completion timeline of 36 months. Under Clause 14.2 of the agreement, failure to achieve 40% physical progress within 18 months entitled the employer to issue a 30-day cure notice prior to invoking contractual termination.) ' 
(3. On 10 November 2017, the respondents issued a termination notice alleging that the appellant had achieved merely 14.8% progress. The appellant contended that delays were attributable to reciprocal defaults of the State in handing over unencumbered right of way \(ROW\) across 4.2 kilometers of forest land, and non-clearance of statutory environmental clearances under the Forest \(Conservation\) Act, 1980.) ' 
(4. The primary questions of law framed for consideration before this Court are:) ' 
(\(i\) Whether the High Court, exercising extraordinary writ jurisdiction under Article 226 of the Constitution of India, ought to entertain a writ petition arising out of a contractual dispute against the State or its instrumentalities where reciprocal obligations and disputed questions of fact are alleged;) ' 
(\(ii\) Whether the contractual termination without adherence to the mandatory 30-day cure period stipulated under Clause 14.2 constitutes arbitrary state action violating Article 14 of the Constitution.) ' 
(5. Mr. K.V. Vishwanathan, learned Senior Counsel appearing for the appellant, vehemently contended that even in realm of public contracts, the State is bound by the mandate of fairness under Article 14. Reliance was placed on the decision in ABL International Ltd. v. Export Credit Guarantee Corporation of India Ltd. \(2004\) 3 SCC 553, to submit that writ jurisdiction is not completely excluded merely because the dispute involves a contractual element.) ' 
(6. Per contra, Mr. Tushar Mehta, learned Solicitor General appearing for the respondent Corporation, urged that the dispute involves complex engineering milestones and reciprocal contractual breach which can only be adjudicated via the arbitration clause \(Clause 21\) provided in the Concession Agreement.) ' 
(7. Having considered the rival submissions and examined the record, we are of the considered opinion that while judicial review in contractual matters is circumscribed, the State cannot act with unbridled arbitrariness. Where the contract itself prescribes a pre-condition of a 30-day cure notice, such clause is mandatory and creates a legitimate expectation of compliance. The failure to issue a cure notice vitiates the impugned termination order.) ' 
(8. In our considered view, the ratio decidendi is that public authorities discharging contractual functions are bound by procedural fairness under Article 14 of the Constitution. When a public commercial agreement specifies an express prerequisite cure notice period before termination, unilateral termination in disregard of such contractual condition is arbitrary and amenable to judicial review under Article 226.) ' 
(9. Consequently, the Civil Appeal is allowed. The judgment of the High Court dated 14 March 2019 is set aside. The order of termination dated 10 November 2017 is quashed, and the respondents are directed to restore the site status quo and permit adjudication of pending reciprocal claims in accordance with the arbitration mechanism under Clause 21. No order as to costs.) ' 
ET

